10:33:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily. Direct damage to the **spinal cord, brain, pelvis, nerves to the legs, or leg muscles/bones** is a major reason someone may need extensive PT to walk again—but it is not the only main reason. After a serious torso or abdominal gunshot wound, a person may have intact legs and an intact spinal cord yet still need PT because they are temporarily unable to walk normally due to: - profound whole-body weakness after blood loss, ICU care, infection, or prolonged bed rest; - painful abdominal/back surgery and weak core muscles needed to stand upright; - reduced balance and endurance; - nerve irritation or injury outside the spinal cord; - fear of moving or falling after a traumatic injury; - altered posture and movement habits developed to protect the wound. In that situation, PT is often about **regaining** walking capacity rather than recovering from leg paralysis. Someone may be medically able to move their legs but too weak, painful, unsteady, or deconditioned to safely walk independently. So: if someone cannot walk because their legs do not receive or produce normal movement signals, direct injury to the spinal cord, brain, peripheral nerves, pelvis, or legs is often the key issue. But if they can move their legs and just cannot stand or walk well after a severe torso injury, the limiting factors may mainly be pain, core impairment, illness-related weakness, and deconditioning.
