10:34:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Basic teaching often emphasizes the most intuitive examples: fractures, hip surgery, joint replacement, back/spine conditions, stroke, and neurologic injury. Those are important and common reasons for gait-focused PT. But standard nursing and rehabilitation teaching is broader than “PT after a hospital stay is about leg or spine damage.” It also includes **deconditioning** (loss of strength and endurance from illness or inactivity), postoperative mobility limits, impaired balance, pain, ICU-acquired weakness, and safe transfers/ambulation after abdominal, thoracic, cardiac, or other major surgery. For example, a patient after major abdominal surgery may be referred to PT to assess whether they can safely get out of bed, stand, walk in the hall, climb stairs, and go home—even if their legs and spine are uninjured. The goals can include preventing complications of immobility, rebuilding endurance, teaching safe movement around an incision, and restoring independent mobility. So the accurate “standard teaching” is: - **Leg/hip/spine/neurologic damage** is a common direct cause of needing gait rehabilitation. - **Serious illness, surgery, trauma, and hospitalization** can also cause temporary inability to walk safely and are routine reasons for PT. Neither point cancels the other.
